## 5️⃣ Create a reusable movie row

```swift
struct MovieRowView: View {
    let movie: Movie

    var body: some View {
        HStack(spacing: 12) {
            Image(systemName: movie.genre.icon)
                .font(.title2)
                .foregroundColor(movie.genre.color)
                .frame(width: 44)

            VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 3) {
                Text(movie.title)
                    .font(.headline)
                    .foregroundColor(
                        movie.isWatched ? .secondary : .primary
                    )

                Text("\(movie.genre.rawValue) • \(movie.year)")
                    .font(.caption)
                    .foregroundColor(.secondary)
            }

            Spacer()

            VStack(alignment: .trailing, spacing: 4) {
                Text(String(format: "%.1f", movie.rating))
                    .font(.caption)
                    .fontWeight(.bold)

                if movie.isWatched {
                    Image(systemName: "checkmark.circle.fill")
                        .foregroundColor(.green)
                }
            }
        }
        .padding(.vertical, 4)
    }
}
```

### 📖 Theory: View and body

```swift
struct MovieRowView: View
```

This declares a SwiftUI view. The `View` protocol requires a `body` property that describes what the interface should look like.

```swift
var body: some View
```

`some View` means the property returns a value conforming to `View`, while allowing the compiler to infer its concrete type. A SwiftUI view can combine many nested views into a single expression.

### 📖 Understand the layout containers

- `HStack` arranges children **horizontally**.
- `VStack` arranges children **vertically**.
- `Spacer()` takes available space and pushes surrounding content apart.
- `Image(systemName:)` displays an SF Symbol.
- `Text` displays text.
- `.padding()` adds space around content.
- `.font`, `.foregroundColor` and `.frame` are **view modifiers** that change appearance or layout.

SwiftUI builds the interface by composing these small views. It isn't the same as manually placing every pixel.

### 📖 Understand the ternary operator

```swift
movie.isWatched ? .secondary : .primary
```

This is shorthand for an if/else expression:

- If `isWatched` is true, use `.secondary`.
- Otherwise, use `.primary`.

The row dims the title of a watched movie. The `if movie.isWatched` block also shows a checkmark only when the movie has been watched.

