### 📖 Theory: how does `@Bindable` enable `$vm.title`?

The ViewModel is stored with `@State`, but the form controls need **bindings** to its properties.

```swift
@Bindable var vm = viewModel
```

This local declaration exposes binding access to the existing observable object. It doesn't create a new object.

Then:

```swift
TextField("Movie Title", text: $vm.title)
```

uses `$vm.title` as a two-way connection between the text field and the ViewModel property.

Without the binding, the text field wouldn't have the required mechanism to write changes back to the model.

### 📖 Theory: Picker and enum selection

```swift
Picker("Genre", selection: $vm.selectedGenre) {
    ForEach(Genre.allCases, id: \.self) { genre in
        Label(genre.rawValue, systemImage: genre.icon)
            .tag(genre)
    }
}
```

The selection is a binding to the selected enum case.

`ForEach` creates an option for every genre. `.tag(genre)` associates each displayed option with its actual `Genre` value, so selecting the option updates `selectedGenre`.

The `id: \.self` syntax means each enum value acts as its own identity.

### 📖 Theory: what is `Form`?

`Form` is a SwiftUI container designed for data-entry interfaces. It groups controls into sections and applies a platform-appropriate form layout.

The controls in this form have different roles:

- `TextField` accepts a single line of text.
- `Picker` selects a genre.
- `Stepper` increments or decrements a number.
- `Slider` selects a value within a range.
- `TextEditor` accepts multiline text.
- `Section` groups related controls.

### 📖 Theory: why use a closure for `onSave`?

```swift
var onSave: (Movie) -> Void
```

This closure accepts a `Movie` and returns `Void`. The parent supplies its implementation when presenting the form.

```swift
onSave(viewModel.buildMovie())
```

The form creates the movie and reports it to the parent. It does not need a direct reference to `MovieListViewModel`, so the two ViewModels remain separate.

### 📖 Theory: why does Cancel not save anything?

```swift
Button("Cancel") {
    dismiss()
}
```

The Cancel button only dismisses the form. It never calls `onSave`.

The Save button builds a movie, sends it to the parent and dismisses. That separation is what makes cancelling a form safe.

