### 📖 Theory: how the search binding works

```swift
.searchable(
    text: $vm.searchText,
    prompt: "Search movies…"
)
```

The search field needs a binding to the `searchText` property.

When the user types, the binding updates the ViewModel. Since `filteredMovies` is computed from `searchText`, the array returned to `ForEach` changes and the visible list updates.

The View uses:

```swift
ForEach(viewModel.filteredMovies) { movie in
```

rather than `viewModel.movies`, so it renders only the results returned by the filtering logic.

### 📖 Theory: what is `ForEach`?

`ForEach` generates a view for each element in a collection. Because each `Movie` conforms to `Identifiable`, SwiftUI knows which element corresponds to each row.

`List` provides the scrollable list container, while `ForEach` generates the rows. This combination also lets the tutorial attach swipe-to-delete behaviour to the generated rows.

### 📖 Theory: what does `.onDelete` do?

```swift
.onDelete {
    viewModel.deleteMovies(at: $0)
}
```

SwiftUI supplies an `IndexSet` representing the rows selected for deletion. The closure passes those offsets to the ViewModel's deletion method.

The View doesn't directly remove anything from the array. It communicates the user's intent; the ViewModel performs the operation.

### 📖 Theory: what does `.animation` do?

```swift
.animation(
    .default,
    value: viewModel.filteredMovies.map { $0.id }
)
```

The `map` closure transforms the filtered movie array into an array of IDs. The animation modifier uses that value to detect changes in the displayed items and apply a default animation.

It doesn't perform the filtering. It only affects how changes are **presented**.

### 📖 Theory: why use `@ToolbarContentBuilder`?

```swift
@ToolbarContentBuilder
private var toolbarContent: some ToolbarContent
```

The toolbar is a special kind of SwiftUI content. `@ToolbarContentBuilder` lets the computed property construct toolbar items and then be used by `.toolbar`.

The plus button toggles `showAddMovie`, and the settings button toggles `showSettings`. These are view-owned presentation states, so `@State` is appropriate.

### 📖 Theory: how do the sheets communicate with the parent?

```swift
.sheet(isPresented: $showAddMovie) {
    AddMovieView {
        viewModel.addMovie($0)
    }
}
```

The parent presents the sheet and supplies a callback. The callback invokes `addMovie` on the parent ViewModel.

The sheet doesn't know where the movie will be stored. It only knows it must call `onSave` with a `Movie`.

The settings sheet doesn't need that callback because it accesses shared `UserPreferences` from the environment.

### 📖 Theory: why is the navigation title computed?

The title depends on the display name. A computed property keeps that logic out of the main view layout:

```swift
private var navigationTitle: String {
    preferences.displayName.isEmpty
        ? "Movie List"
        : "\(preferences.displayName)'s Movies"
}
```

If the name is empty, the app displays "Movie List". Otherwise, it includes the name.

