# Part 6 — The theory you should know across the entire codebase

This section connects the concepts that appear in multiple files. It is particularly useful for understanding unfamiliar SwiftUI code in a practical or lab assessment.

## 2️⃣5️⃣ The five most important property-wrapper patterns

### 1. `@State` — ownership

```swift
@State private var viewModel = MovieListViewModel()
```

The view **owns** the stateful reference. SwiftUI preserves that state across ordinary redraws of the view.

### 2. `@Observable` — change tracking

```swift
@Observable
class MovieListViewModel { ... }
```

Makes the object's properties observable, so SwiftUI can react to relevant changes.

### 3. `@Bindable` — two-way bindings

```swift
@Bindable var vm = viewModel
TextField("Movie Title", text: $vm.title)
```

Provides binding access to the properties of an observable object.

### 4. `@Environment` — shared dependencies

```swift
@Environment(UserPreferences.self) private var preferences
```

Retrieves a shared object supplied through the environment.

### 5. `@Environment(\.dismiss)` — presentation control

```swift
@Environment(\.dismiss) private var dismiss
```

Retrieves SwiftUI's dismissal action, used to close a sheet or dismiss the current presentation.

> ⚠️ A common beginner mistake is treating all five as interchangeable. They are not:
>
> - `@State` manages view-owned state.
> - `@Observable` enables change tracking.
> - `@Bindable` enables two-way property bindings.
> - `@Environment` retrieves dependencies from the environment.
> - `dismiss` is an action provided by the environment.

