## 2️⃣6️⃣ Operators and syntax that look complicated

| Syntax | Meaning | Example in your app |
|---|---|---|
| `?` | Optional type or optional chaining, depending on context | `onMarkWatched?` |
| `!` | Boolean NOT or forced unwrap, depending on context | `!isFormValid` |
| `$` | Binding projection in SwiftUI | `$vm.searchText` |
| `{ ... }` | Closure body or computed-property body, depending on context | `filter { ... }` |
| `$0` | First closure argument | `viewModel.addMovie($0)` |
| `->` | Function return type | `(Movie) -> Void` |
| `:` | Type annotation, argument label, or dictionary separator depending on context | `var title: String` |
| `&&` | Logical AND | Form validation |
| `==` | Equality comparison | Matching movie IDs |
| `...` | Closed range, including both endpoints | `1...500` |

### One example that combines several concepts

```swift
movies.firstIndex(where: { $0.id == movie.id })
```

Read it in plain English:

> Search the movies array and return the first index where the current element's ID matches the supplied movie's ID.

That one expression uses an array method, a closure, shorthand argument syntax, property access and equality comparison.

Don't try to memorise it as a mysterious block. Learn what each piece contributes.

